# {{ title }}

YAMD is safe to render, even when a document comes from someone you don't trust.

## HTML is sanitized

HTML renders like it does on GitHub: an allow-list of tags and attributes is kept, and everything else is removed. The script, the event handler and the frame below are all dropped; only a harmless image tag is left:

<script>alert("hello")</script>
<img src=x onerror="alert(1)">
<iframe src="https://example.com"></iframe>

Allowed tags work as expected: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>, H<sub>2</sub>O, <ins>inserted</ins>.

<details>
<summary>Click to expand</summary>

Collapsible sections use GitHub's `<details>` tag.

</details>

Comparisons need no escaping: if a < b and c > d, then a <= b.

## Trusted HTML only

Styles and other HTML outside the allow-list only render inside `:::html`, and only when the renderer is in trusted mode. The preview is never in trusted mode, so this is shown as source:

:::html
<div style="color: red">Hello from HTML</div>
:::

## Allowed URLs

Links and images only keep these kinds of URL:

| Scheme      | Example                              |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `https`     | [secure site](https://example.com)   |
| `http`      | [plain site](http://example.com)     |
| `mailto`    | [send email](mailto:a@example.com)   |
| `tel`       | [phone us](tel:+15551234567)         |
| Relative    | [the tables example](../01-basics/tables.yamd) |

Images may also use `data:image` URLs:

![A small gray square](data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciIHdpZHRoPSIxNiIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxNiI+PHJlY3Qgd2lkdGg9IjE2IiBoZWlnaHQ9IjE2IiBmaWxsPSIjOGM5NTlmIi8+PC9zdmc+){width=16}

Anything else, such as `javascript:` links, is removed and flagged. The [diagnostics example](diagnostics.yamd) shows one.

## Comments stay private

%% Internal: the launch date is still under NDA. %%

The comment above is removed when parsing, so it isn't in the page source either.
